Paragraph  vice-president of the United States and a member of the Democratic Party. He was born on November 4, 1963 in New York City to Barack Obama Sr., an economist from Kenya, and Stanley Ann Dunham, an anthropologist from Kansas. His parents divorced when he was two years old, and his mother moved with him to Indonesia for a period of time before returning to Hawaii.

Obama's early life in Hawaii was marked by the influence of both his African heritage and American upbringing. He attended Punahou School, an elite private school on Oahu, where he excelled academically and athletically. After graduating from high school, Obama moved to Los Angeles to attend Occidental College for two years before transferring to Columbia University in New York City, where he graduated with a Bachelor of Arts degree in political science.

After college, Obama worked as a community organizer in Chicago's South Side neighborhoods, focusing on issues such as housing and job training programs. He later enrolled at Harvard Law School, where he became the first African American president of the Harvard Law Review. After graduating with a Juris Doctor degree magna cum laude, Obama moved to Chicago to work as a civil rights attorney.

In 1991, Obama was elected to the Illinois State Senate as a Democrat. He served in the state senate for eight years before being elected to the United States Senate in 2004. During his time in the U.S. Senate, he gained national attention for his opposition to the Iraq War and his advocacy for healthcare reform.

In 2008, Obama announced his candidacy for president of the United States as a member of the Democratic Party. He won the Democratic nomination after a hard-fought primary campaign against Hillary Clinton. In the general election, he faced off against Republican candidate John McCain and won with 365 electoral votes to McCain's 173.

As president, Obama signed into law several major pieces of legislation, including the Affordable Care Act (also known as "Obamacare"), which expanded access to healthcare for millions of Americans; the Dodd-Frank Wall Street Reform and Consumer Protection Act, which aimed to prevent another financial crisis like the one in 2008; and the Don't Ask, Don't Tell Repeal Act, which ended the military's ban on openly gay service members.

Obama was re-elected as president in 2012, defeating Republican candidate Mitt Romney with a larger margin than his first victory. During his second term, he focused on issues such as climate change, immigration reform, and criminal justice reform. He also played a key role in negotiating the Iran nuclear deal and normalizing relations between Cuba and the United States.

Obama's presidency was not without controversy. His handling of the 2016 presidential election, which saw Donald Trump elected as his successor, drew criticism from some quarters. Additionally, his use of executive orders to bypass Congress on certain issues sparked debate over the limits of presidential power.

On January 8, 2017, Obama left office and was succeeded by Donald Trump. He currently resides in Washington D.C. with his wife Michelle Obama and their two daughters, Malia and Sasha.
